> let f of x be x squared plus 1
  ucas: f(x) = x^2 + 1
  ucas: show f
f(x: real) = x^2 + 1
  -> real
> call it g
  ucas: g(x: real) = f(x)
  ucas: show g
g(x: real) = x^2 + 1
  -> real
> what is f of 3?
  ucas: f(3)
10
> what is g of 3?
  ucas: g(3)
10
> what is its range?
  ucas: range g
range of g:
  [1, 101]   min at x ≈ 0, max at x ≈ -10
  grid of 20001 points over [-10, 10] (default; give x a range), extrema refined at critical points
> let f of x be x cubed plus 2
  ucas: f(x) = x^3 + 2
  ucas: show f
f(x: real) = x^3 + 2
  -> real
> what is f of 3?
  ucas: f(3)
29
> what is g of 3?
  ucas: g(3)
10
> call it h
  ucas: h(x: real) = f(x)
  ucas: show h
h(x: real) = x^3 + 2
  -> real
> what is h of 2?
  ucas: h(2)
10
> define k of x as x squared minus 4
  ucas: k(x) = x^2 - 4
  ucas: show k
k(x: real) = x^2 - 4
  -> real
> what are the roots of k?
  ucas: zeros k
zeros of k:
  2 zeros:
    x = -2
    x = 2
  proved: a polynomial of degree 2 has 2 roots counted with multiplicity
> what is k of 5?
  ucas: k(5)
21
> what is its derivative?
  ucas: diff k
d/dx k = 2x
  holds for every real x
  check: matches central differences at 201 points (max relative difference 2e-15)
> f of 5
  ucas: f(5)
127
> what is f of g of 2?
  ucas: f(g(2))
127
